WEBVTT

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:05.934
A fellow knitter recently showed me
a ready-to-wear sweater

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.440
that she wanted to duplicate.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.720
Now, anytime I look at sweaters
with my machine knitter's eye,

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.160
I put them to the test.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.080
Can it be done?

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:20.880
Our machines have some limitations
and we need to keep these in mind.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.200
Is it practical?

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.760
Some designs just don't lend
themselves to our knitting machines.

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:31.560
Sweaters knit in the round, for example.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.960
Are you willing to do what it takes?

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.400
One knitter may find it great fun to hand
manipulate every square inch of a sweater.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:46.560
Others, like me, prefer an easier route.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.600
Let's take a look at the construction
of this sweater and consider

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.440
these three criteria.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:59.720
This design is a fitted cardigan
in a tunic length with short sleeves.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:06.440
It has empire styling and the soft
collar frames a very low V neck.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.374
It's difficult to see the armholes
in the photo, but I'm guessing

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:12.760
it has set in sleeves.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.000
It looks like the knitting is plain old
stockinette and it has ribbed hems

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:21.880
other than the collar.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:25.160
It appears that this sweater
meets our first criteria.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:26.520
Can it be done?

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:27.720
Sure.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.840
The second criteria is,
is this design practical on the machine?

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:41.160
Now, it appears that the collar is
the only area that could be problematic.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:46.134
Because of our needlebed limitations,
we couldn't knit the entire collar

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.280
and front bands in one piece.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:54.240
Now that we've established that it's
possible, the big question is,

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.760
are you willing to do what it
takes to create this design?

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:10.334
First, are you willing to draft
the complete pattern from scratch or are

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.440
you willing to modify an existing pattern?

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:15.840
Here are some options.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:22.400
You could start with a knit it now basic
pattern for a cardigan with set in sleeves

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:28.160
and use our tools to modify it to create
that V neck and the other

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:29.760
pieces of this pattern.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.174
If you have DesignaKnit, you could create

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:40.614
the pattern shapes if you're so inclined.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:44.934
You could draw the shapes to scale
based on the finished dimensions

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:50.680
of the sweater and create a design
for your knit leader or knit radar.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:58.054
You could also find a hand or machine
knitting pattern that is close to this

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:01.560
shape and make changes for your design.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.240
Do you have a ribber?

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:11.640
If not, the hems could be hand
manipulated or knit by hand.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:18.280
Let's tackle the most difficult part of
this design, the collar and the neckband.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:24.120
The easiest solution would be to pick up
and knit the collar and bands by hand.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:26.200
Do you hand knit?

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:28.760
Are you willing to take time to do it?

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:34.614
Now, with a little planning,
we could knit the ribbed band

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.680
and the collar in multiple pieces.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:41.160
It would be important to plan where
the seams would be placed

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:44.520
and notice that the collar is shaped.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:49.174
It's wider at the center back,
so there would be some short

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.000
row shaping in the collar area.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:54.680
Hmm.
Short rows in ribbing.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:02.360
For me, creating this sweater would be
a fun challenge, especially

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:04.040
because I love to hand knit.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:05.960
What about you?

